Yeah currently we can't promise full support for pyright, because we don't test it. We've discussed adding pyright tests, but there are already some challenges in supporting two different type checkers (mypy and pytype), and adding a third to the mix would likely cause even more maintenance headaches.
@superbobry has advocated in the past for switching one of our current type checkers to pyright – I'm not sure where we stand on that, but I tend to trust anything he says about Python types & type checking! I'd be curious to hear his thoughts here.
In any case, I think we'd happily accept a PR fixing this or any other typing issue, with the understanding that such fixes are sometimes difficult to land: we have pytype test jobs that check JAX against a number of downstream packages, and so type changes in JAX (even obviously correct ones!) often necessitate downstream fixes before we can merge them.

