@ivanstepanovftw Notice that in the reference implementation, the following if-block
elif z < info.min:
        return info.min
is never executed because with int8 x and uint32 y (always positive!), z is never smaller than info.min.
In general, the overflow from addition x + y can be easily detected as follows:
if x > 0 and y > MAX_VALUE - x:
  # x + y will overflow
elif x < 0 and y < MIN_VALUE - x:
  # x + y will underflow
else:
  # x + y does not over/underflow
that in your case needs to be adjusted to take into account mixed input types.
Assuming signed x dtype and unsigned y dtype, try the following JAX implementation
def sat_add(x, y):
    info = np.iinfo(x.dtype)
    mxp1 = y.dtype.type(-info.min)
    mx = x.dtype.type(info.max)
    ux = x.astype(y.dtype)
    return jnp.where(y > mxp1 - ux, mx, (ux + y).astype(x.dtype))
(it passes test_sat_add test).
Notice that this solution relies on that uint32 overflow (that appears in mxp1 - ux when x is negative) has the same behavior as int32 overflow. UPDATE: int32 overflow is UB. Nevertheless, a possible uint32 overflow in mxp1 - ux when it appears, has the desired behavior.

