It sounds like if we want JAX code to be compatible with the array API when used with transformations like jit and vmap, we need tracer.device to be implemented and to return some value indicating that the concept of "device" is meaningless in the given context. @yashk2810 what do you think?

This is basically what I hacked in data-apis/array-api-compat#238.
The problem with it is that it causes the device of the output arrays to be unpredictable, whereas most users would expect it to match the input's.
The pattern affected is this:
@jax.jit
def f(x):
    xp = array_namespace(x)
    if some_fast_exit_condition:
        return xp.zeros(1, dtype=x.dtype, device=x.device)  # Or asarray, or ones, etc.
    else:
        return g(x)
See https://github.com/search?q=repo%3Adata-apis%2Farray-api-extra+device%3D_compat.device&type=code for two distinct examples of this pattern in action.
With my current workaround, if x is not on the default device then the output of f(x) will be sometimes on x.device and some other times on the default device.

