A traced value is a value that is only available at runtime, in the context of an XLA operation. A PyTree is a Python data structure, which is only available at trace-time. XLA has no conception of a "PyTree" – XLA only knows about operations on arrays. This is why a tracer cannot be used to index into a PyTree – the traced index and the pytree exist at different levels of the stack, and cannot directly interact.
That said, you can make this work if you convert  your pytree into some kind of structure that can be represented in XLA.
One way to do this would be to stack the contents of the selection into a single array, which XLA can then index into at runtime:
@jax.jit
def get_asarray(collection, idx):
    return jnp.stack(collection)[idx]

collection = (jnp.asarray((0, 1)), jnp.asarray((0, 1)))

print(get_asarray(collection, 0))
# [0 1]
Another would be to use the lax.switch operation. This effectively maps your Python tuple onto a tuple of functions that XLA is able to index into at runtime:
@jax.jit
def get_switch(collection, idx):
  funcs = [lambda i=i: collection[i] for i in range(len(collection))]
  return jax.lax.switch(idx, funcs)

print(get_switch(collection, 0))
# [0, 1]

