Well, in most cases of interest the compilation time should be small compared to the runtime. Your example here is somewhat of a worst case, because it uses Python for loops (which are unrolled by the compiler) over very cheap operations, so the output is dominated by compile time. If this is really reflective of the kind of problem you're trying to solve, my recommendation would be to not use jit. If this is not reflective of the kind of problem you're trying to solve, then you're probably in the regime where you don't really have to worry about compilation time. What do you think?
Side-note, I'm not sure it's relevant to your true problem of interest, but for best results you should avoid for loops and use vectorized operations directly. For example, this is the vectorized equivalent of your original function, and should have at least 10x faster compilation & execution time.
@jax.jit
def some_func(vec, indices):
    out = jnp.zeros(len(indices))
    return out.at[indices].set(vec[:len(indices)])

